Title: "Semantics of First-Order Logic: Structures and Satisfaction"
Id: "90325db2-66f2-40b1-88d5-ff674dab6a06"


Text: "Syntax defines the combinatorial universe of formulas; semantics assigns
       truth values to those symbolic forms. In propositional logic, truth
       valuations assigned truth values directly to propositional atoms. In
       first-order predicate logic, statements assert properties of individual
       objects and relationships among them. Consequently, first-order semantics
       requires specifying a universe of objects—a structure—together with an
       interpretation mapping symbols to entities, functions, and relations."
Id: "e7358686-e10c-485f-9e11-2716cfe9a712"


[\fol.structure]
Declares: M
Documented:
. called: "first-order structure"
. written: "\mathcal{M}"
. description: "A first-order interpretation structure comprising a non-empty
                domain of discourse together with interpretations for constant,
                function, and predicate symbols."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{80}
Id: "b38b6dcc-ac7c-4b19-8dcc-45eb0f68cddc"


[\fol.domain{M}]
Defines: D is \set
when: M is \fol.structure
Documented:
. called: "domain of discourse of $\mathcal{M}?$"
. written: "|\mathcal{M}?|"
. description: "The non-empty underlying set of objects over which variables and
                quantifiers in structure \mathcal{M} range."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{80}
Id: "97d2459a-9abe-49f9-bf28-52f084be0224"


Text: "A structure interprets each non-logical symbol in the signature: every
       constant symbol c denotes a specific element in the domain, each n-ary
       function symbol f denotes an actual operation from the n-fold Cartesian
       product of the domain into the domain, and each n-ary predicate symbol P
       denotes an n-ary relation on the domain."
Id: "6f327333-bc73-49a2-b4fd-9fd4264fca27"


[\fol.interpretation{M}]
Declares: I
when: M is \fol.structure
Documented:
. called: "interpretation mapping of $\mathcal{M}?$"
. written: "I^\mathcal{M}?"
. description: "The mapping that assigns concrete domain elements, operations,
                and relations to non-logical symbols in structure \mathcal{M}."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{80}
Id: "d029e240-0588-443b-a53a-4ee714787614"


Text: "Because first-order formulas may contain free variables, the truth of an
       expression cannot be determined in isolation from variable assignments. A
       variable assignment function s maps the infinite set of all individual
       variables into the domain of the structure."
Id: "9bce35b3-3c43-446b-be46-2a34f63eaddc"


[\fol.assignment{M}]
Declares: s
when: M is \fol.structure
Documented:
. called: "variable assignment into $\mathcal{M}?$"
. written: "s: \text{Var} \to |\mathcal{M}?|"
. description: "A function assigning a concrete element of domain |\mathcal{M}|
                to each individual variable."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{83}
Id: "fcf67774-a97f-4284-8ac2-41af9f264303"


Text: "Every term represents an object in the domain. Given a variable
       assignment s, the denotation of a term is computed recursively: variables
       evaluate according to s, constants evaluate according to their
       interpretation in the structure, and composite terms evaluate by applying
       function interpretations to subterm values."
Id: "6a6956df-3faa-4183-917f-603e021473c5"


[\fol.term.value{t}:in{M}:under{s}]
Declares: val
when:
. t is \fol.term
. M is \fol.structure
. s is \fol.assignment{M}
Documented:
. called: "term evaluation $\bar{s}(t)$"
. written: "\bar{s}?(t?)"
. description: "The unique element of |\mathcal{M}| obtained by recursively
                evaluating term t in structure \mathcal{M} under assignment s."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{83}
Id: "e0e94825-7453-4b82-9ad4-d7bc26ae6e04"


Text: "In 1933, Alfred Tarski formalized the semantics of truth for formal
       languages by defining the satisfaction relation inductively on formula
       complexity. A structure satisfies a formula under assignment s if the
       condition asserted by the formula holds in the interpretation."
Id: "d7e4275d-5f0f-4c7c-a7e6-3c9e0edc32f8"


[\fol.truth{phi}:in{M}:under{s}]
States:
when:
. phi is \\statement
. M is \fol.structure
. s is \fol.assignment{M}
that:
. have: (phi is \\statement)
Documented:
. called: "satisfaction $\mathcal{M} \models \varphi[s]$"
. written: "\mathcal{M}? \models \varphi?[s?]"
. description: "Tarski's satisfaction relation stating that structure
                \mathcal{M} satisfies formula \varphi under variable assignment
                s."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{84}
Id: "d81d8382-7586-48a6-a2d6-1b2e860cc695"


Text: "When \sigma is a sentence (possessing no free variables), satisfaction is
       entirely independent of the variable assignment: either \mathcal{M}
       satisfies \sigma under all assignments, or under none. When satisfied, we
       state that \mathcal{M} is a model of \sigma."
Id: "c921f1a3-f147-42c6-bab7-63fa7bb58fb1"


[M \.models.fol./ sigma]
States:
when:
. M is \fol.structure
. sigma is \fol.sentence
that:
. forAll: s is \fol.assignment{M}
  then: \fol.truth{sigma}:in{M}:under{s}
Documented:
. called: "model relation $\mathcal{M} \models \sigma$"
. written: "\mathcal{M}? \models \sigma?"
. description: "Structure \mathcal{M} is a model of sentence \sigma, meaning
                \mathcal{M} satisfies \sigma under every variable assignment."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{88}
Id: "61b8691f-9868-43ef-8d46-ed9e9989c5dd"


Text: "A formula is logically valid if it is true in every first-order structure
       under every variable assignment. Logically valid formulas are the
       first-order counterparts to propositional tautologies, representing
       universal truths of pure logic."
Id: "bd67d256-041a-4528-85e9-2093fe8ab76e"


[\fol.valid]
Declares: phi is \\statement
satisfies:
. forAll: M is \fol.structure
  then:
  . forAll: s is \fol.assignment{M}
    then: \fol.truth{phi}:in{M}:under{s}
Documented:
. called: "logically valid formula"
. written: "\models \varphi?"
. description: "A first-order formula that is true in every structure under
                every variable assignment."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{88}
Id: "28635b0c-9251-4aae-8ec5-80d474655f54"


Text: "A formula or set of formulas is satisfiable if there exists at least one
       structure and variable assignment under which it evaluates to true."
Id: "6dd9702d-4b9d-4d62-9943-8601b129d8ec"


[\fol.satisfiable]
Declares: phi is \\statement
satisfies:
. exists: M is \fol.structure
  suchThat:
  . exists: s is \fol.assignment{M}
    suchThat: \fol.truth{phi}:in{M}:under{s}
Documented:
. called: "satisfiable first-order formula"
. description: "A formula that is satisfied by at least one structure under at
                least one variable assignment."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{88}
Id: "447569b3-4e16-4737-af02-b9cc48c23edb"


Text: "A formula \varphi is a semantic consequence of a theory \Sigma, written
       \Sigma \models \varphi, if every structure and assignment that satisfies
       all sentences in \Sigma also satisfies \varphi."
Id: "0008bf76-baf5-4239-9d88-cdec8adb67c6"


[Sigma \.semantic.entails./ phi]
States:
when:
. Sigma is \set
. phi is \\statement
that:
. forAll: M is \fol.structure
  then:
  . forAll: s is \fol.assignment{M}
    then: \fol.truth{phi}:in{M}:under{s}
Documented:
. called: "semantic consequence $\Sigma \models \varphi$"
. written: "\Sigma? \models \varphi?"
. description: "The relation asserting that every model of theory \Sigma is also
                a model of formula \varphi."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{88}
Id: "1e902afa-ae63-4cae-9505-a70082883f21"


Theorem:
given:
. M is \fol.structure
. sigma is \fol.sentence
then:
. (sigma is \\statement)
Documented:
. called: "bivalence of first-order truth for sentences"
. description: "For any first-order structure \mathcal{M} and sentence \sigma,
                the sentence is either satisfied by \mathcal{M} under all
                assignments or refuted under all assignments."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{86}
Id: "26784b1a-e888-4527-8e9e-7e48554470ca"
